there steve kaufman here today i want to  tell you  the secret of how i learn languages  that's the subject of my discussion  today  people often say to me  you know steve you've obviously got a  talent for languages  you've learned so many languages you  know good for you but i could never do  it and i often say to myself at least  well why don't you try doing it the way  i do it  maybe  it's also a matter of the method that i  use  and so i want to tell you exactly now  because it's really quite simple  the secret to how i learn languages to  me  learning languages is is essentially the  i would summarize it this way it's the  relentless pursuit of words  words that i glean  through content that is of interest to  me  that's basically what it boils down to  uh if i look at the languages that i  have learned french in a way doesn't  count because i had it at school and  then i studied it at mcgill and then i  went to france where i studied  in french at the institute politik and  so i was you know eventually was  immersed in it as i say in my book i  really couldn't speak much in french but  once i got turned down to french it  wasn't difficult in montreal and  subsequently in france to achieve quite  a high level the significance with  french is that it made me confident that  i could learn but in chinese i remember  when i was in hong kong i would scour  the bookstores  looking for material that had these  glossaries like readers  um readers on chinese literature  politics history anything i could find  any book that anybody put out that had  some kind of content with a glossary so  i wouldn't have to use the dictionary i  would buy them and and that i didn't  just use the textbook that they had at  my chinese language school and so i was  on chinese for nine months and and  that's but essentially i between that  and listening a lot that's how i learned  and and i as i have said many times i  ignored the grammatical explanations and  just got used to the patterns  in japanese i had to learn it on my own  and there again i would just scour the  bookstores for as much material as i  could find  so this whole idea that that language  learning is a matter of pursuing words  okay it's it's just that's very simple  and that's what we have put into link  and i get kind of annoyed sometimes  that you know at link i'm one of the  most  prolific linkers like i do more saving  of words and phrases than most people  uh i'm very disappointed in this because  to me  the fastest way to learn is to create a  lot of links to do a lot of reading  and then while reading to just  link words at random i mean i've been at  czech for  two months not quite two months and i've  created over 10 000 links  when i sit down to read some things  especially at my level in check if i go  to a newspaper there's a lot of words  that i don't know i'm just creating  links and i don't worry about whether i  remember the meaning  just to explain when i create a link i  have the dictionary the  dictionary definition comes up i save it  to my database  and  i'll just collect them by the thousands  and  when i read again i see them highlighted  in yellow it reminds me that i i looked  them up but by no means do i know those  words so that linking is just  a way of making much more automatic  what was my essential learning process  and that is acquiring words  and yet people are reluctant to do that  if they say 10 20 words they think they  have to learn them memorize them make  sure they know them  do i really know this word is it my  passive only vocabulary how do i make it  active  you know is it masculine feminine what  case is it i don't worry about that i  just pile the words up  and then every so often i go through  them as i'm doing now in check  and i'm looking at them carefully and  i'm tagging them which is something we  can do in link so i'm tagging them for  case because i want to become a little  more  uh attentive you know as i've said  before language learning is attitude  time with the language and attentiveness  so i have to become a little more  attentive to what's happening so i'm  going to focus on case for a while and  so because i'm  quite  you know  having a clue what the different case  endings in in check are which e  and which ones they are and and so forth  it's different from russian obviously  and so i'll do a bit of that and that  helps me become attentive and then i  just go right back to creating links  and and to me the way we design link is  we like to keep track of how many links  have you created how many words do you  know the way we count them and here  again people get so uptight you know if  i  because one of our learners was doing  korean and he was surprised that i had  saved so many links in czech and he  hadn't saved as many in korean and even  though he had studied korean longer so i  went in and did a lesson in korean and  in an hour i saved 160  links or so  uh just to to demonstrate that if i'm  going to go at it i'm going to save  links now the words that you don't save  those are considered known  so it's very possible that in the  so-called known words there are words  that you skipped over words that you  don't really know  names and all kinds of stuff it doesn't  matter it's a measurement of your  progress it doesn't mean like according  to my statistics that link i know 13 000  check words i don't know 13 000 czech  words however  i can just i can make my way through the  newspaper i've been at it for two months  and the vocabulary is quite different  from russian  and so i feel that i'm making  because link is so much more efficient  now than than it was when i started with  russian i mean i'm making so much faster  progress now than i was making before  but the key element is that that the  what you have to do in line with  learning in my opinion is you have to go  after words  words that you acquire through reading  occasionally you review them i don't  because i only have  an hour an hour and a half a day i can't  spend three hours with my anki  uh flash guard deck  you know if i got 10 000 words  so i prefer to review these in a  sporadic fashion tagging them for cases  and then i want to get right back again  to reading  creating links and then listening  because once i've finished my work at  the computer then i've got something  that i can listen to in the car wherever  i am but the fundamental point is that  and and you know i'm reluctant sometimes  to stress this because you know  everybody says well i got my own way and  people feel very strongly about their  own way of language learning and you  know fine they have every right to to do  that  uh i mean we have you know some people  think that that you can talk your way to  fluency in three months well i don't  believe that  uh but some people are happy  at least trying to do that  and if you're happy with the process  then it's good because again  i do believe that it's attitude  it's time with the language and and it's  attentiveness that matter  but  sometimes i think you know maybe i'm not  the least successful language learner so  maybe some people might listen  to what i do  and not just say oh well you're good at  it  i'm going to go back to this other way  that doesn't work  because i'm no good  well maybe you're not very effective  because the method you're using isn't  very effective  and maybe i sometimes think at link we  should set the target for linking much  much higher than we do  much much higher and try and sort of  promote the ideas go and massively  link  multi-link you know  anyway i just felt that i should say  that because i i  i don't want to force my way on others  although i do  you know  because i really think it works and i  think language learning is actually  quite simple it's a bit like as i often  say  the dog races  the dogs chasing the mechanical rabbit  the mechanical rabbit in this case is  the word count and so you chase the word  count you build up the word count which  forces you like the dog who's running it  forces you to read it forces you to  listen you're building up your word  count and all of a sudden you start to  become familiar with the language and  then you can go back in and check up a  few things that relate to grammar and  then when you have some words then you  feel more comfortable comfortable in  engaging people in conversation  but you don't have to do it right away  what you have to do right away is you  have to  single-mindedly pursue words and i can  think of no more efficient way of doing  that than creating  lots of lots of links at link so there  you have it that's the secret of how i  learn languages so some people are going  to say well he learned all of his  languages before like that's true link  is simply a more efficient way  of doing what i have always done  to learn languages  thanks for listening bye
 

